│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multistart_conflict_local_search                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the sampled append-only heuristic with a cached multi-start greedy search         │
│                          │ followed by conflict-aware local optimization.                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. The previous implementation only samples up to ten possible next transactions and      │
│                          │ ignores `num_seqs`. It can therefore miss the transaction that produces the lowest actual │
│                          │ partial makespan.                                                                         │
│                          │ 2. Each construction now evaluates every remaining transaction using                      │
│                          │ `workload.get_opt_seq_cost`, which directly measures the objective rather than relying on │
│                          │ operation counts or write counts.                                                         │
│                          │ 3. The first construction is fully greedy, while later constructions choose from a small  │
│                          │ restricted candidate list of near-best choices. This preserves a strong deterministic     │
│                          │ baseline while exploring alternative conflict orientations that may lead to better final  │
│                          │ schedules.                                                                                │
│                          │ 4. Every unique evaluated ordering is cached. Prefixes and local-search candidates recur  │
│                          │ frequently, so this substantially reduces repeated simulator calls.                       │
│                          │ 5. The strongest constructed schedules are improved using relocation and swap descent.    │
│                          │ These moves can repair bad early choices by moving a transaction before or after          │
│                          │ conflicting transactions, something append-only greedy construction cannot do.            │
│                          │ 6. For very small workloads, all permutations are evaluated, guaranteeing the optimal     │
│                          │ transaction ordering rather than merely a heuristic result.                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Because every selection and local move is accepted only when the simulator reports a      │
│                          │ lower makespan, the returned schedule is never worse than the best constructed candidate  │
│                          │ and will generally reduce conflict-induced delay.                                         │
