│ patch_description        │ This version combines the stronger beam/VND approach from the current implementation with │
│                          │ the sampled greedy construction strategy from the crossover program.                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key improvements:                                                                         │
│                          │ 1. Exact enumeration remains enabled for very small workloads.                            │
│                          │ 2. Beam initialization evaluates all possible starting transactions instead of randomly   │
│                          │ discarding promising starts.                                                              │
│                          │ 3. Construction uses a GRASP-style restricted candidate list: it evaluates conflict-aware │
│                          │ prefix makespans, normally selects the best candidate, but sometimes chooses among        │
│                          │ near-best candidates to diversify schedules.                                              │
│                          │ 4. Multiple sampled greedy constructions are retained as independent seeds.               │
│                          │ 5. Local search combines adjacent swaps, sampled/general swaps, insertions, and segment   │
│                          │ reversals. Adjacent swaps are always examined because local conflict inversions are       │
│                          │ especially common in transaction ordering.                                                │
│                          │ 6. An iterated perturb-and-rebuild step allows refined schedules to escape local minima   │
│                          │ while preserving much of a good transaction ordering.                                     │
│                          │ 7. Exact schedule-cost caching avoids repeatedly simulating identical schedules.          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The resulting implementation spends more evaluations on decisions that directly affect    │
│                          │ makespan rather than relying on transaction length or write-count heuristics.             │
