╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_segment_reversal_vnd                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ Add a deterministic segment-reversal neighborhood to the existing variable-neighborhood   │
│                          │ descent.                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Step-by-step rationale:                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. The current search evaluates single-transaction relocations and pairwise exchanges.    │
│                          │ These moves repair isolated ordering mistakes but can remain trapped when several         │
│                          │ transactions participating in the same read/write dependency chain need to change their   │
│                          │ relative order together.                                                                  │
│                          │ 2. A contiguous reversal changes the precedence relationship of every pair within a       │
│                          │ congested region in one evaluated move. This is especially useful when greedy             │
│                          │ construction placed a group of writers/readers in approximately the opposite              │
│                          │ dependency-friendly order.                                                                │
│                          │ 3. Each reversal is scored by `sequence_cost`, which calls the real simulator cost rather │
│                          │ than a transaction-size or conflict-count proxy. Therefore, the move is accepted only     │
│                          │ when it directly reduces makespan.                                                        │
│                          │ 4. This is a complete deterministic O(n²) neighborhood, substantially cheaper than        │
│                          │ exhaustive multi-transaction insertion while complementing the existing random            │
│                          │ destroy-and-repair phase. It can also provide a better incumbent before                   │
│                          │ destroy-and-repair begins.                                                                │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
